 (doc "Tuna book chapter: replay — verification, divergence detection, and the debugging surface. Replay is a consistency proof between the pure core and the journal, not a debugging afterthought.")
 (status implemented)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section replay
  (doc "Re-executing recorded runs: faithful replay for verification, live replay for debugging, counterfactual replay for exploration; divergence reported as structural diffs designed for agents.")

  (subsection faithful
   (doc "Faithful replay — the verification primitive")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Faithful replay of a run: load program hash + input hashes,
         execute under the canonical strategy, answer every prim call
         SEQUENTIALLY from the run's journal (journal.recorded-
         environment), and compare against the run row:

           1. result tree hash equality
           2. step count equality      (SPEC.md §6.2 — replay identity)
           3. per-seq journal result hashes equality (accepted answers
              consumed in order; mismatch = divergence point)
           4. journal hash chain intact

         A run verifies iff all four hold. Verification is therefore a
         free integrity check on BOTH interpreter and data: a bit flip
         in stored programs, a corrupted journal, an interpreter
         regression, and host tampering all surface as verification
         failures, distinguished by which invariant broke.

         Step-count equality is load-bearing AND contested: any
         optimization of the evaluator (hash-consing, memoized
         reduction, sharing) changes the step accounting unless the
         canonical strategy defines the count. v1 carries the ruling:
         the count reported and verified is defined by the canonical
         interpreter; optimized engines must either reproduce it
         exactly or run under an explicit "optimized" flag whose runs
         are marked non-verifiable. Choosing the optimized flag often
         is the acceptance.failure-definitions trigger for the research
         finding "step counts did not survive optimization".
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection live
   (doc "Live replay — a debugging mode, not verification")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Live replay re-executes against the CURRENT world (prim calls
         go to the live host under fresh grants). It answers "would
         this run still work today" — regression detection across host
         upgrades — and never writes verification state. Live replays
         mint their own run rows + journals (provenance link to the
         parent run), so the world-diff between original and live journal
         is itself diffable: "what changed since this run" becomes a
         journal-versus-journal structural diff, the operations analog
         of program first-diff.

         Live replay is explicitly NOT replay-verification: under live
         replay neither step counts nor result hashes are invariants,
         only diagnostics are.

         Implementation (server/lib/replay.ml Replay.live_replay): the
         parent's program + inputs are re-executed through the SAME run
         boundary (Run.execute) against the live host under caller-
         supplied grants, minting a NEW run row whose parent_run_id is
         the original.  The new row is never given a verify_status.
         The world-diff is journal-versus-journal (diff_journals):
         align the recorded and live journals by seq and name the first
         changed answer (result_hash | error | prim | extra | missing);
         tree-shaped changes carry first_diff_path; a pure run with no
         changes reports a null diff.  Surface: POST
         /api/runs/:id/live-replay {"grants":[ids],"fuel","size_cap"}
         -> {run, live_replayed_from, world_diff, contract_mismatches,
         journal}.  Grant denial at a prim boundary journals the denial
         (never an exception), exactly like a normal run.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection divergence-surface
   (doc "First-divergence reporting: diffable, not readable")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Design target: the debugging surface is indexed structure for
         agents, not prose for humans. Agents do not read transcripts;
         they grep structure. Every verification failure reports JSON:

           { "run": <id>,
             "divergence_seq": <journal seq of first mismatch>,
             "callsite_path": <program tree path at that seq>,
             "prim": ..., "recorded_result_hash": ...,
             "replayed_result_hash": ...,
             "first_diff_path": <path into the differing trees>,
             "tree_path_span": {...call-sites.provenance map entry...} }

         first_diff_path is a structural tree walk (per-path subtree
         hashes make it cheap: descend where child hashes disagree, stop
         at first), and callsite_path routes through call-sites.
         provenance to the source span as a human courtesy. The stack-
         trace analogue here is not a wall of text — it is an addressed
         diff. Acceptance requires the computed first_diff_path to match
         a hand-derived path on a fixed corpus (acceptance.criteria),
         because a divergence detector nobody has hand-checked is
         decoration.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection prim-versioning
   (doc "Pinning the prim contract: what a journal does and does not pin")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         A journal pins what the host RETURNED, under prim_contract and
         host_build recorded per row (journal.row-schema) — never what a
         prim SHOULD have returned. Consequences:

           - Faithful replay is unconditional: the recorded answers are
             data; replaying against them never depends on current host
             behavior. Verification CANNOT rot as the host evolves.
           - Live replay and counterfactual interpretation DO depend on
             the current contract; the divergence surface reports
             contract mismatches as first-class fields
             ("recorded under prim X v2, current v3") so an upgrade is
             distinguishable from a regression.
           - Silent semantic drift is the enemy: prims that change
             behavior without a contract bump are definitionally
             cheating. The prim layer needs a discipline as strict as
             the calculus side: contract bump or it did not change.

         Boundary creep watch (research lens): if, after v1, ~all
         meaningful work lives in prims and the tree layer is thin
         ceremony, that is the acceptance.failure-definitions "thesis
         inversion" finding — the security came from the prim layer
         all along. The versioning discipline is what makes that finding
         MEASURABLE rather than anecdotal.

         Implementation: Prims.contract is the SINGLE source (currently
         "2"); the boundary (Run) pins it on every journal row and
         nothing else writes a literal.  A row records prim_contract and
         host_build; Replay.contract_mismatches compares each row's
         pinned contract to Prims.contract, and the divergence/live-
         replay JSON carries recorded_contract, current_contract,
         contract_mismatch, and the recorded build as first-class fields
         ("recorded under prim X contract vN (host_build B), current
         vM"), so an upgrade is distinguishable from a regression.  A
         mismatch count is read as the length of the
         contract_mismatches array on a live replay (zero for rows this
         build wrote); it rises only when prims changed behavior under
         an unchanged contract — definitionally cheating — or when a
         genuine contract bump lands.  Faithful replay remains
         unconditional: the recorded answers are data, so a run verifies
         under any current contract (asserted in tests/store_tests.ml).
    |))
   )
  )
 )
)
